    {
      "format": "National Dex Monotype",
      "outdated": null,
      "overview": "",
      "comments": "",
      "movesets": [
        {
          "name": "Sticky Web (Bug)",
          "pokemon": "Galvantula",
          "shiny": false,
          "gender": "DC",
          "levels": [],
          "description": "<p>Galvantula serves as an excellent Sticky Web lead, supporting its offensive team members with speed control. Thunder in conjunction with Compound Eyes gives Galvantula an offensive presence, takes on opposing hazard-setting leads, threatens paralysis, and dents Water- and Flying-types. Energy Ball threatens bulky Ground-types like <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/great-tusk/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Great Tusk</a>, <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/hippowdon/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Hippowdon</a>, <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/swampert/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Swampert</a>, and <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/gastrodon/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Gastrodon</a>. Hidden Power Ice deals with Flying- and Dragon-types like <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/gliscor/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Gliscor</a>, the <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/landorus/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Landorus</a> formes, <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/garchomp/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Garchomp</a>, and <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/dragonite/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Dragonite</a>. Thunder Wave can be used over Hidden Power Ice to paralyze setup sweepers and fast threats that can't be effectively slowed down with Sticky Web, most notably Dragonite, <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/zapdos-galar/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Galarian Zapdos</a>, <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/aerodactyl-mega/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Mega Aerodactyl</a>, opposing <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/pinsir-mega/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Mega Pinsir</a>, and <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/volcarona/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Volcarona</a>, without having to rely on Thunder to do so. This comes at the cost of losing coverage against Pokemon that resist both Thunder and Energy Ball, however. Sticky Web lowers the amount of Quiver Dance boosts needed for Volcarona to outspeed <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/regieleki/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Regieleki</a>, Unburden <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/sneasler/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Sneasler</a>, and Sand Rush <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/excadrill/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Excadrill</a>, making its setup more efficient. Sticky Web also allows choice Scarf users like <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/kleavor/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Kleavor</a> and <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/buzzwole/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Buzzwole</a> to outspeed those Pokemon, limiting the amount of revenge killing options against them and improving their effectiveness against Fire- and Rock-types by taking care of Choice Scarf <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/blacephalon/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Blacephalon</a>, <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/arcanine-hisui/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Hisuian Arcanine</a>, and <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/iron-boulder/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Iron Boulder</a>. Sticky Web gives Mega Pinsir more opportunities to use Return instead of Quick Attack to increase its damage output or Earthquake to KO Pokemon that would usually threaten it like Choice Scarf <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/nihilego/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Nihilego</a> and Hisuian Arcanine. <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/scizor/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Scizor</a> can OHKO or force <a href=\"/dex/sv/pokemon/diancie/national-dex-monotype/\" hreflang=\"en\">Diancie</a> out before it can Mega Evolve to allow Galvantula to set up Sticky Web against Fairy- and Rock-teams.</p>",
